text:
I need a JSON object for a dashboard widget I'm building. Pull the following data from live sites right now (today is 2026-09-21). Do NOT guess — visit each site and read the actual values.

1. formula1.com → go to the Drivers' Standings page for the 2026 season. I need the top 6 drivers: name, team, points. Also find the total number of races scheduled for 2026 by checking the Schedule page.

2. postgresql.org/docs/ → find the exact latest patch version string from the news announcement banner (e.g. something like '18.6'). Also find the total number of manual PDFs offered (count all A4+US PDF links for every version listed, including beta). Also find how many translated manual languages are linked at the bottom.

3. letterboxd.com → search for the film 'Senna' (the 2010 documentary). I need: its rating (number out of 5), the number of ratings (integer), and the year of release shown on the page.

Output strictly as valid JSON with this exact schema:
{
  "f1": {
    "top6": [{"rank":1,"driver":"...","team":"...","points":N}, ...],
    "races_2026": N,
    "leader_gap_to_2nd": N
  },
  "postgresql": {
    "latest_patch": "...",
    "total_pdf_links": N,
    "translated_languages": ["...", ...]
  },
  "letterboxd": {
    "film": "Senna",
    "year": N,
    "rating": X.X,
    "num_ratings": N
  }
}

If any value cannot be found, set it to null and add a "notes" field explaining why.

As a data analyst I need to verify how JSON-to-XML conversion handles nested structures. Go to https://jsonformatter.org/json-to-xml and input this JSON:

{"company":"Acme Corp","departments":[{"name":"Engineering","headcount":45},{"name":"Marketing","headcount":23},{"name":"Sales","headcount":31}]}

Click Convert. In the resulting XML output, report: (1) the total number of XML elements (opening tags) visible in the output, (2) the value of the <headcount> element for the 'Marketing' department, and (3) whether the output includes an XML declaration line like <?xml version="1.0"?>. Provide the source URL used for the conversion.

I want to build a small JSON artifact and validate it using https://jsonformatter.org/json-parser. Please do the following: navigate to https://jsonformatter.org/json-parser. In the editor, enter this intentionally broken JSON that is missing a closing bracket: {"items":[{"product":"Widget","price":4.99},{"product":"Gadget","price":12.50}

Observe what the parser reports — specifically the error message text it displays (copy it verbatim). Then fix the JSON by adding the missing closing bracket ]} at the end so it becomes: {"items":[{"product":"Widget","price":4.99},{"product":"Gadget","price":12.50}]}

Re-parse it. Confirm it validates without errors. Then report: (a) the exact error message shown for the broken version, (b) whether the corrected version parses successfully with no errors (true/false), and (c) the total number of key-value pairs visible in the tree view of the corrected JSON (count each key like 'items', 'product', 'price' as one pair each, counting all occurrences).

Prepare my Monday morning briefing. Output strictly in the JSON format I specify below. Steps:

1. Go to https://fred.stlouisfed.org and note the 'Trending Search Terms' listed on the homepage. Then search for 'inflation' and record the top 3 series titles that appear in results.

2. Go to https://www.abebooks.com and search for 'economics' as a keyword. Sort by price (highest first) if that option exists; otherwise just note the results order. Extract the first 5 results: title, author, price with currency, and whether 'Rare' or 'First Edition' appears in the listing.

3. Go to https://www.theverge.com and record the first 8 'Top Stories' or 'Latest' headlines visible on the homepage, along with the author names shown.

Output format — return a single JSON object:
{
  "fred": {
    "trending_terms": ["..."],
    "inflation_top_series": ["title1", "title2", "title3"]
  },
  "abebooks": {
    "search_term": "economics",
    "results": [{"title": "", "author": "", "price": "", "is_rare_or_first": true/false}, ...]
  },
  "the_verge": {
    "headlines": [{"title": "", "author": ""}, ...]
  }
}
Do not include any text outside the JSON object.

I need a quick JSON summary for a dashboard widget. Go to clinicaltrials.gov and get the count of trials with status 'Recruiting' and phase 'Phase 2'. Then go to usajobs.gov and count results for keyword 'data science'. Then go to rust-lang.org and get the current stable version string. Output EXACTLY this JSON and nothing else:

{
  "clinical_trials": {
    "site": "clinicaltrials.gov",
    "filter": "status=Recruiting, phase=Phase 2",
    "count": <integer>,
    "source_url": "<full URL of results page>"
  },
  "federal_jobs": {
    "site": "usajobs.gov",
    "filter": "keyword=data science",
    "count": <integer>,
    "source_url": "<full URL of results page>"
  },
  "rust_stable": {
    "site": "rust-lang.org",
    "version": "<string e.g. 1.98.1>",
    "source_url": "https://www.rust-lang.org"
  }
}

Do not add commentary. Just the JSON.

I'm putting together a competitive pricing research dossier for a demo and need you to do a full crawl of the Web Scraper test-site ecosystem at https://webscraper.io/test-sites. I need data from three sites compiled into one report.

STEP 1: Crawl https://webscraper.io/test-sites/pagination — visit every page in every category, click into each product detail page, record Name, Category, Price, Rating (out of 5), Review Count, and the full list of spec key-value pairs.

STEP 2: Crawl https://webscraper.io/test-sites/load-more — click 'Load More' until all items are visible in each category, open each product, record the same fields.

STEP 3: Crawl https://webscraper.io/test-sites/scroll — scroll to the bottom until no new items load in each category, open each product, record the same fields.

STEP 4: Compile a final deliverable in strict JSON with this top-level structure:
{
  "total_products_scraped": <int>,
  "products": [
    {
      "name": "<string>",
      "source_site": "<pagination|load-more|scroll>",
      "category": "<string>",
      "price": <number in USD>,
      "rating": <number 0-5>,
      "reviews": <int>,
      "specs": { "<key>": "<value>", ... }
    },
    ...
  ],
  "summary": {
    "cheapest_by_category": { "<category>": { "name": "<string>", "price": <number> } },
    "highest_rated_by_category": { "<category>": { "name": "<string>", "rating": <number> } },
    "price_range_by_site": { "<site>": { "min": <number>, "max": <number> } }
  }
}

Sort the products array by category alphabetically, then by price ascending within each category. Use fake test data (name: Test User) if any form interaction is needed. Do not skip any products — completeness is critical.

Quick job for you — on epochconverter.com, convert the following three timestamps (in seconds) to human-readable dates using the 'Time stamp to readable date' field. Enter each one, read the result, then move to the next. Report back in this exact JSON:

{"1893456000": "YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS UTC", "1704067200": "YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS UTC", "1789964349": "YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS UTC"}

Make sure you read the UTC column from the site's output.

Open https://app.diagrams.net. If asked to choose storage, pick 'Decide Later' or 'Device'. Once the canvas is up, look at the bottom-left status bar (or the page properties) to read the default page dimensions. I need the answer in this exact JSON structure — nothing else:
{"page_width_px": <number>, "page_height_px": <number>}
If the dimensions are shown in a different unit (mm, in, cm), convert to pixels (1 in = 96 px, 1 cm = 37.7953 px, 1 mm = 3.77953 px) and still report in px. Do not add any commentary.

Go to https://www.demoblaze.com and browse the 'Phones' category. I need you to identify the most expensive phone and the cheapest phone currently listed there. Add both to the cart. Then report the result in this exact JSON format:

{"most_expensive": {"name": "...", "price": ...}, "cheapest": {"name": "...", "price": ...}, "price_difference": ..., "cart_total": ...}

Prices should be numeric (no dollar sign). price_difference = most_expensive.price - cheapest.price. cart_total should match what's shown in the cart.

Use https://play2048.co to play one complete game session. Start by clicking 'Try again' to get a fresh board. Play by pressing arrow keys in any direction you choose, but stop immediately once the score reaches 100 or higher OR the game is over—whichever happens first. Count each key press as one move. Report your final result in this exact JSON format:
{"moves": <int>, "score": <int>, "max_tile": <int>, "stop_reason": "score_reached"|"game_over", "board_occupied": <int>}
where board_occupied is the count of tiles currently on the 4x4 grid.

I need a JSON report. Do exactly the following and return ONLY valid JSON at the end — no prose before it except the word 'REPORT' on a single line.

Step 1: Visit nasa.gov. Navigate to the Image of the Day section. Record the image title and the mission/spacecraft it is credited to. Then go to the News & Events section and record the titles of the 3 'Featured' articles shown on the homepage (the ones under the 'Featured' heading).

Step 2: For each of those 3 featured article titles, search Brave for the exact title plus 'details'. Then search again for 'Khan Academy' + a keyword from the article's topic. Visit khanacademy.org and confirm whether a course covering that keyword exists. Record: confirmed (true/false), course name if found, or null.

Step 3: The 3 featured articles on the NASA homepage (as of when you visit) will each have a topic (e.g., SpaceX crew flight, baby planet discovery, Moon crater). For each topic, go to newegg.com and search for a product that would help someone interested in that topic (e.g., telescope, simulation software, VR headset, astronomy kit, or relevant hardware). Click into the product to get: exact product title, current price, and the 'Ships From' or shipping info if visible. Pick one product per topic.

Step 4: Compile into this JSON schema:

REPORT
{
  "image_of_the_day": {
    "title": "string",
    "credited_mission": "string"
  },
  "featured_articles": [
    {
      "title": "string",
      "topic_keyword": "string",
      "khan_academy": {
        "confirmed": true|false,
        "course_name": "string|null"
      },
      "newegg_product": {
        "title": "string",
        "price_usd": number,
        "ships_from": "string"
      }
    }
  ],
  "sites_consulted": {
    "nasa_gov_pages_visited": number,
    "brave_searches_performed": number,
    "khanacademy_org_pages_visited": number,
    "newegg_com_pages_visited": number
  }
}

Make sure featured_articles has exactly 3 elements. All prices as numbers without dollar signs. Output the JSON and nothing else after the closing brace.

Navigate to the XYZ Bank demo at https://www.globalsqa.com/angularJs-protractor/BankingProject/. Go to the Manager section and open the customer list. Extract the table data and return it in this exact JSON format:

