I'm a research librarian preparing a web-content curation report for my institution's 'Science of the Ancient World' resource guide, launching October 2026. I need you to scrape specific data points from five websites and return a single JSON object. No extra commentary — just the JSON. Use today's live data (September 21, 2026).

Here are the exact data points to collect:

```json
{
  "smithsonian_articles": [
    {
      "url": "<full URL>",
      "title": "<headline>",
      "author": "<byline or null>",
      "date": "YYYY-MM-DD",
      "section": "<Smart News|Science|History|Innovation>",
      "topic": "<one of: paleontology|architecture|medieval|optics|dinosaurs>"
    }
    // Include all articles on the current homepage under Science, Smart News, or History that relate to paleontology, dinosaurs, ancient architecture, medieval history, or ancient optics. Expect 5+ entries.
  ],
  "openlibrary_books": [
    {
      "title": "<title>",
      "author": "<primary author>",
      "year": <integer or null>,
      "editions": <integer>,
      "borrowable": <true|false>,
      "subject_tag": "<primary subject heading found>"
    }
    // Search 'dinosaur', 'parthenon', 'medieval manuscripts', 'ancient architecture', and 'paleontology'. Collect up to 20 unique books total (deduplicate by title+author).
  ],
  "nps_parks": [
    {
      "park_name": "<full official name>",
      "state": "<two-letter code>",
      "fee_adult": "<dollar amount or 'free' or 'varies'>",
      "has_dinosaur_content": <true|false>,
      "has_geology_content": <true|false>,
      "current_alerts": <integer>,
      "url": "<park URL>"
    }
    // Check these specific parks: Dinosaur National Monument, Petrified Forest National Park, Badlands National Park, Craters of the Moon National Monument, Florissant Fossil Beds National Monument, Hagerman Fossil Beds National Monument, John Day Fossil Beds National Monument, Mesa Verde National Park, Canyon de Chelly National Monument, and Arches National Park. Visit each park page.
  ],
  "worldbank_indicators": [
    {
      "indicator_name": "<exact name>",
      "indicator_code": "<e.g., NE.FOR.TOTL>",
      "country": "United States",
      "year": <integer>,
      "value": <number>,
      "unit": "<unit string>"
    }
    // Find US data for: Forest area (% of land area), Protected areas (% of terrestrial territory), and any indicator mentioning 'fossil' or 'geological' if available under Planet theme. Record the most recent year available for each.
  ],
  "semanticscholar_papers": [
    {
      "title": "<paper title>",
      "authors": ["<author1>", "<author2>"],
      "year": <integer>,
      "venue": "<journal or conference or null>",
      "citation_count": <integer>,
      "url": "<full URL>"
    }
    // Search: 'theropod warm-blooded dinosaur body temperature', 'Parthenon optical illusion mathematics', 'medieval virus ancient DNA', 'sturgeon longevity vertebrate', 'Tyrannosaurus physiology'. Collect top 10 most-cited per query, deduplicate. Expect 30+ entries.
  ]
}
```

Return ONLY the valid JSON object. Do not add markdown fences. Do not add explanatory text. Ensure all numbers are actual numbers (not strings), booleans are booleans, and nulls are nulls. If a field cannot be found, use null. If a fee is 'varies', that exact string. Every URL must be a full https:// URL.

Go to https://ecommerce-playground.lambdatest.io, scroll down to the 'Top Products' section on the homepage, and add the first two products to the shopping cart using the 'Add to Cart' button. Then navigate to the cart page. Report the result in this exact JSON format:
{"products": [{"name": "...", "price": "..."}, {"name": "...", "price": "..."}], "subtotal": "...", "total": "..."}
Use the prices as displayed in the cart (with dollar sign and two decimal places). If the two products happen to have the same name, still list them as two separate entries with their respective quantities shown in the cart.

