I need a deliverable table for a client report. Use epochconverter.com to produce the following. DO NOT summarize or abbreviate values — I need exact numbers from the pages.

FORMAT: Output everything in a single JSON object with these exact keys:

{
  "epoch_now": <the current Unix epoch seconds shown on the homepage at https://www.epochconverter.com>,
  "epoch_now_ms": <the milliseconds value also shown on the homepage>,
  "epoch_now_us": <microseconds>,
  "epoch_now_ns": <nanoseconds>,
  "date_2024_leap": {
    "input_seconds": <epoch seconds for 2024-02-29 12:00:00 UTC, computed from the /date-calculator page which you must use to generate it>,
    "week_number": <week number of that date from /weeknumbers or /weeks/2026 — actually use the date-difference or the calendar to verify; just note what /weeknumbers shows for that date if it has a calculator, otherwise compute from /calendar/2026>,
    "hex_timestamp": <from /hex>,
    "snowflake_id": <from /snowflake>,
    "julian_day": <from /julian-day>,
    "seconds_since_y0": <from /seconds-days-since-y0>
  },
  "countdown_test": {
    "target_date": "2027-01-01 00:00:00 UTC",
    "seconds_remaining": <value shown on /countdown for that target>
  },
  "timezone_offsets": {
    "city_1": {"name": <first city name>, "utc_offset": <offset string>},
    "city_2": {"name": <second city name>, "utc_offset": <offset string>},
    "city_3": {"name": <third city name>, "utc_offset": <offset string>}
  },
  "programming_snippet_count": <total number of distinct programming languages or systems listed on /programming/>,
  "year_2038_date": <the exact date shown on /year-2038-problem when the overflow occurs>
}

Instructions: (1) Go to homepage, read all four epoch values. (2) Go to /date-calculator, enter 2024-02-29 and time 12:00:00 UTC to get the epoch seconds. (3) Use that epoch seconds value on /hex, /snowflake, /julian-day, /seconds-days-since-y0. (4) Go to /calendar/2026 to find what week number Feb 29, 2024 would be — if the calendar only shows 2026, instead use the homepage date-to-timestamp in reverse or state the week number you can determine from /weeks/2026 pattern; if you cannot determine the week number for 2024 from the available tools, set "week_number" to null. (5) Go to /countdown, set target to 2027-01-01 00:00:00 UTC, record seconds. (6) Go to /world-clock and record the first 3 cities with their UTC offsets. (7) Go to /programming/ and count total language/system entries. (8) Go to /year-2038-problem and note the exact date. (9) Assemble the JSON. Return ONLY the JSON object, no prose.

I need a cross-reference table for an internal briefing. Produce a JSON object with the exact keys and nested structure below. Populate every field from what is currently visible on the live sites. If a value cannot be determined, use the string "unavailable".

{
  "cdc": {
    "topic_page_url": "full URL of the H5 Bird Flu topic page",
    "symptoms": ["list every symptom term exactly as written on the page"],
    "outbreak_listed": true/false,
    "outbreak_details": {"source": "...", "date": "YYYY-MM-DD or unavailable", "status": "..."},
    "a_z_index_contains": true/false
  },
  "sciencedaily": {
    "articles": [
      {"headline": "...", "date": "YYYY-MM-DD", "summary_first_sentence": "..."}
    ],
    "total_found": <int>
  },
  "bookshop": {
    "searches": [
      {"query": "avian influenza", "results": [{"title": "...", "author": "...", "format": "...", "price_usd": <float>}]},
      {"query": "bird flu", "results": [...]},
      {"query": "zoonotic disease", "results": [...]}
    ],
    "unique_book_count": <int after dedup by title>
  },
  "lastfm": {
    "search_queries_tried": ["H5N1", "Avian Flu", "bird flu", "bird"],
    "artist_found": "<exact artist name as displayed>",
    "listeners": <int>,
    "scrobbles": <int>,
    "tags": ["..."]
    "top_tracks": [{"track": "...", "plays": <int>}]
  }
}

Instructions for you:
1. On cdc.gov, navigate to the H5 Bird Flu page via the homepage link. Extract symptoms verbatim. Then go to /outbreaks/index.html to check for H5 or bird flu entries. Confirm the A-Z index includes 'H5 Bird Flu'.
2. On sciencedaily.com, scan the homepage and use any available search to find articles mentioning H5, bird flu, avian, or influenza (research context). Collect every match visible within the first 3 pages of results or the homepage topic listings.
3. On bookshop.org, run three searches sequentially. Deduplicate books by title (case-insensitive) for the unique_book_count.
4. On last.fm, try each search term in order. Stop at the first query that returns an artist page. Extract all fields from that artist page.

Output ONLY the JSON object. No commentary before or after.

